<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runs"
      updated="August 23, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"the-list", "The Runs list"},
        {"statuses", "Run statuses"},
        {"run-detail", "A single run"},
        {"output", "Live output and caps"},
        {"cancelling", "Cancelling a run"},
        {"operations", "Operations and retries"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runs">
        <:dek>
          Use the Runs page to see what your fleet did. It lists every action dispatched to a host,
          with its requester, result, and policy decision.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="the-list">The Runs list</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each row is one invocation against one host: the time, action ID, target runner,
        dispatcher, status, and duration. Rows update live as a run moves through its
        lifecycle, so you can watch a dispatch go from queued to finished without reloading.
        On a phone, the first line carries the action and status, and the second the origin,
        runner, and time.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runs.webp"
        title="emisar console · Runs"
        caption="Every dispatch with who asked for it, where it ran, and a color-coded outcome."
        alt="The Runs list: columns for When, Action, Runner, Dispatched by, Status, and Duration. Rows show actions like caddy.reload_config and postgres.replication_lag dispatched across runners edge-fra-01, api-iad-02, and pg-primary-iad, each attributed to a named operator or agent, with color-coded statuses — success, and two runs awaiting approval — and a per-run duration."
      />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Four filters narrow the feed. Each one lives in the URL. The back button, a refresh,
        and a shared link all land on the same view:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Status.</strong>
            One or more lifecycle states. You can pull up just the failures, just what is awaiting
            approval, or just what is still in flight.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Action.</strong>
            A substring match on the action id. Typing
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres</code>
            finds <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.vacuum</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.uptime</code>
            — and
            it is case-insensitive.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runner.</strong>
            A searchable list of your runners, to scope the feed to one host.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Dispatched by.</strong>
            The origin: Operator, AI agent, or Runbook. Select an origin to show a second filter.
            Use that filter to choose a team member, agent, or runbook.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner's page links straight to its own runs. An agent's page links to the runs
        dispatched with its key. The common "what did this host, or this agent, do" question is
        one filtered link, not a hand-built query.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="statuses">Run statuses</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A run moves through a small state machine. It starts <em>pending</em>, becomes <em>sent</em>, then <em>running</em>. From there it settles on exactly one
        terminal outcome. Policy can settle a run before it ever reaches a host. A
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >require-approval decision</.link>
        parks it at <em>awaiting approval</em>, and a deny decision
        ends it at <em>denied</em>.
      </p>
      <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="border-b border-zinc-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Status</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">What it means</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <%= for {status, {_label, meaning}} <- EmisarWeb.RunStatuses.entries() do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap">
                  <.status_badge status={status} />
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Three terminal statuses look similar but call for different responses:
        <.status_badge status={:denied} /> needs a policy change,
        <.status_badge status={:refused} /> usually needs pack trust or signing repair, and
        <.status_badge status={:failed} /> is a real action result, not a gate.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="run-detail">A single run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open a row from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runs</a>
        to see one invocation. The fact strip starts with the outcome — <strong>Status</strong>, <strong>Runner</strong>, <strong>Dispatched by</strong>, <strong>Duration</strong>, <strong>Exit code</strong>, and
        <strong>Started</strong>
        — and the Runner value links to that host.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/run-detail.webp"
        title="emisar console · Run"
        caption="One invocation: the outcome strip, the reason, the exact arguments and executed command, and the bounded output."
        alt="A successful caddy.reload_config run on edge-sfo-03 dispatched by Maya Chen via runbook: the success status with duration and exit code, the Why section with the reason, the JSON arguments, the executed command marked secrets redacted, and the command output."
      />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong>Dispatched by</strong>
        shows the accountable person first. An operator run shows the team member who ran
        it, an agent run shows the human who owns the agent key — with the client as
        secondary context:
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">jordan@example.com via Claude Code</code>
        — and a runbook run shows the runbook. This is the same human-first
        attribution the audit trail uses, so "who ran this, and through what" is answered on the row
        itself.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A <strong>Why</strong>
        section carries the justification. Every dispatch includes a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>
        from the operator or agent. An agent can also include evidence and expected outcome:
        evidence records what it observed, and expected records the outcome that confirms
        success. When
        policy intervenes, the run shows the matching rules and exact policy version. A routine
        allow is omitted because every dispatched run already passed policy.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Below that, <strong>Arguments</strong>
        shows the values passed to the action, with a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sha256</code>
        fingerprint of the exact bytes. The control plane stores the exact arguments and uses
        them for authorization, approval, dispatch, and hashing. UI and API views mask fields
        the action marks as sensitive, and secrets in unmarked fields remain in the stored
        arguments.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong>Executed command</strong>
        shows the shell line the runner ran. The runner applies configured redaction to the
        command and output before they leave the host — a best-effort backstop, not a
        guarantee. How the redaction pipeline works is in the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="output">Live output and caps</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The output panel streams stdout and stderr line by line as the runner produces them. The
        stderr stream uses a separate tint to highlight errors. A <em>streaming</em>
        marker appears while the run is active. A run with no output says so instead of showing an
        empty terminal.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The terminal renders the most recent 500 chunks. A run that streamed more shows a note that
        earlier output was trimmed. This is a
        display limit that keeps a chatty run from growing the page without bound. It caps what is on
        screen, not what the runner sent or what is recorded.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Truncation can also happen at the source. The runner bounds the output it captures per the
        action's output settings. When it hits that limit it clips the stream and flags the run as
        truncated. Each run also records whether every progress chunk arrived. A gap left by a
        mid-run disconnect is visible rather than silent.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every run carries a durable output budget that holds across runner reconnects. A run
        that reaches it stops accepting further chunks, visibly. The numbers are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/limits"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Limits</.link>.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout
        kind={:note}
        title="The Runs page is your live view; the audit trail is the record."
      >
        Run output and events follow your plan's retention window. Export events through the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}>audit trail</.link>
        before the window closes.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="cancelling">Cancelling a run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You can stop a run that has not finished. Open it from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runs</a>
        and use <strong>Cancel run</strong>
        —
        available while the run is pending, sent, or running, to operators and above. What happens
        depends on where the run is:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Queued (pending).</strong>
            The run has not reached the runner yet. It is cancelled immediately and never
            dispatches.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">In flight (sent or running).</strong>
            The control plane signals the runner with SIGTERM, then SIGKILL if needed. The run
            remains at <em>cancelling</em>
            until the runner reports its final outcome. The runner remains the authority on what
            stopped.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Cancellation is recorded in the audit trail and is idempotent: asking to cancel a
        run that already finished does nothing. A run that is <em>awaiting approval</em>
        is not cancelled here — deny its approval instead, which pulls the run back and
        records the denial reason. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >policies and approvals</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="operations">Operations and retries</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar never retries a finished run. Running the action again — from a runner page,
        a runbook, or an agent — is a new run with its own record. If the runner disconnects
        mid-run, the action keeps running and its result arrives on the next connection. If
        it disconnects before dispatch, the run waits in the queue until the runner returns.
        The
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >runner fleet</.link>
        guide covers reconnects and stuck runs.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When an agent sends one action to a runner group, each host gets its own run, and
        emisar groups them under one <strong class="text-zinc-200">operation</strong> — up
        to 16 runners, one result per host. Replaying the operation returns the original
        runs. A lost response never causes a second execution.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runbooks"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">runbook</.link>
        execution is grouped the same way: every run its steps produced, in dispatch order.
        To recover an operation after a lost response, the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>
        has the contract.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Read the status first —
        <a href="#statuses" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Run statuses</a>
        above lists them all, and three are easy to confuse:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Status</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What it means</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What to do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <%= for {status, move} <- [
              {:failed, "Read the output — this is a real result, not a gate."},
              {:error, "Check the runner's connection, then dispatch again once it is back."},
              {:timed_out, "Check the target system — the action was stopped mid-work."}
            ] do %>
              <% {_label, meaning} = EmisarWeb.RunStatuses.entries()[status] %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap">
                  <.status_badge status={status} />
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{meaning}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{move}</td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A queued run is usually not stuck.</strong>
            It waits for its runner and is delivered as soon as that runner reconnects. Only when
            the host is still gone after a short grace window does the sweep end it as
            <.status_badge status={:error} /> rather than leave it queued indefinitely.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A run that ends with
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execution_outcome_unknown</code>
            can have completed its work.</strong>
            The runner reports this when its process or host dies after execution starts,
            before the terminal result was durable. emisar never runs that dispatch again
            automatically — check the target system before you retry.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Output stopped but the run is still running.</strong>
            Output is bounded per action and truncation is reported, so a quiet run is more often a
            quiet command than a lost stream. Check the host with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar events tail -f</code>,
            which shows the local journal live.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact support</strong>
            when the same dispatch reports
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execution_outcome_unknown</code>
            repeatedly, or a run stays <.status_badge status={:running} />
            with no progress while its runner is connected and idle.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
